    "name": "AI Training Data and Annotation",
    "slug": "ai-training-data-and-annotation",
    "count": 31,
    "guide": "A day in this field centers on building and improving the data that trains AI models. That means curating datasets, running labeling pipelines, and doing the human-in-the-loop annotation work that reinforcement learning from human feedback, or RLHF, depends on. People in these roles review model outputs, rank responses, flag errors, and write feedback that gets folded back into training. Quality control sits at the center of the job, since a mislabeled example or an inconsistent rubric can quietly degrade a model for months.\n\nThis work gets confused with Data Engineering and Machine Learning Engineering, but the center of gravity is different. Data Engineering is about building the pipelines, warehouses, and infrastructure that move data around at scale, and Machine Learning Engineering is about building, training, and deploying the models themselves. Training Data and Annotation work sits upstream of both, focused on the raw material models learn from rather than the systems that move it or the models that consume it. Someone in this role might never touch a production pipeline or a model architecture at all, and instead spend their day reading transcripts, tagging edge cases, or tutoring a model in a specific language or subject.\n\nDegrees matter less here than in adjacent engineering roles. Our sample shows almost no formal credential requirements listed in the postings themselves, and where expertise is called out, it is domain expertise, fluency in a specific language, subject matter knowledge in software engineering or the humanities, rather than a computer science degree. No certifications appeared anywhere in our sample, so we are not going to invent one. What gets rewarded instead is demonstrated command of a narrow domain, whether that is a rare language, a technical field, or a specific evaluation skill.\n\nJunior roles in this space tend to be structured around a single narrow task, tutoring a model in one language, reviewing one type of output, applying one rubric consistently. Mid level and senior roles, like the engineering manager position we saw at Datadog focused on AI evaluation and annotation, involve building and running the annotation program itself, setting quality standards, and managing the people or pipelines that produce labeled data. There is not a long, standardized career ladder visible in our data, most of what we tracked sits at a single mid level tier, so it is hard to describe a typical multi year progression with confidence. What we can say is that the jump from doing annotation to managing annotation programs is where seniority shows up in our sample.\n\nNamed tools and platforms are thin in our current sample. A handful of postings list Python, and a few reference large language models and model evaluation as skills directly. Some adjacent postings in our data, a European AI and data consulting role and the Datadog engineering manager posting, list broader stacks like AWS, Azure, Databricks, Spark, and Rust, but these read as general data and cloud skills attached to nearby roles rather than annotation specific tooling. We did not see dedicated labeling platform names appear anywhere in this batch, so we are not going to claim otherwise.\n\nRight now we currently track approximately 29 open postings in this category. The overwhelming majority come from one company, xAI, whose AI Tutor postings span dozens of languages and subject areas, from Yoruba and Hausa to Software Engineering and Audio Editing, all under the same remote, mid level structure. Datadog and a European consulting firm, alixpartners, each contributed a single posting outside that pattern. None of the 46 postings in our full sample had salary_min or salary_max values populated, so we cannot state a salary range for this category from our data.\n\nGiven how concentrated our sample is around one company's multilingual tutoring program, this snapshot says more about the current shape of AI tutor hiring than about training data and annotation work as a whole. Readers should treat the language specific and subject specific breadth as a real signal, since it suggests demand is driven by expanding model capability across new languages and domains. But the absence of salary data, certifications, and named labeling platforms in what we tracked means this guide will get more precise as our sample grows.",
